<div class="art-head"><div><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Notes · Skin concerns · Gut and skin, part 1 · Updated October 2026</div><h1 class="hd">Gut Health and Skin: How Your Gut Shows Up on Your Skin Barrier</h1><p class="serif stand">Your gut and your skin talk to each other all day long. When the gut is out of balance, the skin barrier is often one of the first places I would look: redness, tightness, breakouts, products that suddenly sting. This is part 1 of my skin barrier and gut health series, with the research behind it.</p><div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin-top:10px">glow starts on the inside</div></div>
<div class="ph art-hero"><span>Image 1 · Hero<br><em>Cover collage: smoothie, avocado toast with egg, sheet mask selfie</em></span></div></div></section>
<section class="p art-grid"><div class="art-body">

<p>If your skin flares up when your stomach feels off, you are not imagining a link. Scientists call it the <strong>gut-skin axis</strong>, and it is one of the most discussed ideas in skincare and dermatology right now. The research is growing fast, but it is also easy to oversell: much of it shows <em>associations</em>, not proof that gut changes cause a particular skin problem.</p>
<p>In this note I explain how the gut and skin are connected, what the evidence can and cannot tell us, how a struggling barrier looks and feels, and what I would do first. This is general information from my own reading, not medical advice.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>The gut and skin are linked through the immune system, hormones, nerves and substances made by gut bacteria.</li>
<li>Gut imbalance is associated with acne, eczema, psoriasis and rosacea, but the evidence is mostly observational.</li>
<li>Stress hormones can weaken the skin barrier directly, which is one reason stress shows up on both gut and skin.</li>
<li>Your skin can only use the nutrients your gut absorbs: zinc, omega-3s, vitamin A, vitamin C and amino acids all matter.</li>
<li>The most useful approach is inside and outside: food, sleep and stress care, plus a gentle skincare routine.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What is the gut-skin axis?</h2>
<p>The gut-skin axis is the two-way communication between your digestive tract and your skin. Trillions of microbes live in your gut, and they influence the rest of the body through several routes:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>The immune system.</strong> A large part of your immune system sits around the gut. Gut microbes help train it, and an over-active response can show up as inflammation elsewhere, including the skin.</li>
<li><strong>Microbial by-products.</strong> When gut bacteria ferment fibre they make compounds such as short-chain fatty acids, which are studied for their anti-inflammatory effects.</li>
<li><strong>Hormones and nerves.</strong> The gut and skin both react to stress signals, which is why a stressful week can affect digestion and skin together.</li>
<li><strong>Nutrient absorption.</strong> The gut is where the building blocks for skin repair enter the body.</li>
</ul>
<div class="cmp2">
<div><div class="up">When gut balance is healthy</div><p>the immune response stays calmer and the skin barrier tends to hold up well</p></div>
<div><div class="up">When gut balance is disrupted</div><p>inflammation can rise and the skin tends to become reactive</p></div>
</div>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 2<br><em>A simple gut-skin axis diagram or a gut-friendly breakfast</em></span></div>

<h2>What does the research actually show?</h2>
<p>I want to be honest here, because this topic is full of confident claims. Reviews of the gut-skin axis describe links between the gut microbiome and several skin conditions, including acne, atopic dermatitis (eczema), psoriasis and rosacea. They also describe how diet, probiotics and other gut-directed approaches might change skin outcomes.</p>
<p>At the same time, those reviews point out that many findings come from observational studies, small trials and animal work. That means they show patterns, not that fixing the gut will clear a given skin problem. The probiotics example is a good illustration: a recent review of nine randomized trials in acne reported that lesion counts fell after 12 weeks of oral probiotics, but only slightly at 4 weeks, and the authors say larger trials are still needed.</p>
<blockquote class="quote">The gut-skin axis is a promising way to think about skin, not a magic switch.</blockquote>

<h2>What happens when gut health is compromised?</h2>
<p>A gut imbalance can feed into a weaker skin barrier. Common triggers include:</p>
<ul>
<li>chronic stress</li>
<li>poor digestion or irregular eating</li>
<li>frequent antibiotics</li>
<li>a diet low in fibre and high in highly processed foods</li>
</ul>
<p>Researchers describe a chain where these factors may increase intestinal permeability (often called “leaky gut”), let more inflammatory signals into the bloodstream and keep the immune system on alert. For the skin, the result I would watch for is a barrier that is inflamed, fragile and slower to repair.</p>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">A quick honest note</div><p>Increased intestinal permeability is something scientists measure, but “leaky gut syndrome” is not an accepted stand-alone diagnosis. I use it as a picture of what may be going on, not as a label for what you have.</p></div>

<h2>How gut problems can show up on your skin</h2>
<p>Skin signs linked to internal inflammation can look like this:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>persistent acne, often around the jaw and chin</li>
<li>redness, burning or flushing</li>
<li>eczema or dermatitis</li>
<li>tight, dehydrated skin</li>
<li>sudden sensitivity to products you used to tolerate</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">These are signs that your barrier is struggling to protect and regulate itself, not that your skin is “bad”.</blockquote>
<p>These symptoms have many causes, including genetics, hormones, weather, sun and skincare choices, so the gut is only one piece. If you recognise several of them, it is worth looking at both what you eat and how gentle your routine is. I explain the skincare side in my <a href="/skin-guide/barrier-repair/">barrier repair playbook</a>.</p>
<div class="figrow"><div class="ph fig45"><span>Image 3<br><em>Skin close-up: redness or tight, dry skin</em></span></div><div class="ph fig45"><span>Image 4<br><em>Gentle routine: moisturiser, ceramide cream</em></span></div></div>

<h2>Stress: the link that shows up in both places</h2>
<p>Stress is the part of the gut-skin story I find most practical. A review of 21 studies on stress and the epidermal barrier describes how psychological stress activates the body’s stress-hormone system, and that these hormones can lower the skin’s barrier lipids and structural proteins, reduce hydration and increase water loss through the skin. Stress also changes digestion and gut motility.</p>
<p>So a skin barrier under stress and a gut under stress are often the same story told twice. That is why sleep, rest and slower routines belong in any skin plan, a theme I come back to in <a href="/notes/gut-health-checklist/">my daily gut health checklist</a>.</p>

<h2>Nutrients that support the skin barrier</h2>
<p>Barrier repair also depends on the right building blocks, and your gut is where they are absorbed. These are the ones I focus on:</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>What it does for skin</th><th>Where I look for it</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Zinc</em></td><td>Supports wound healing and recovery after breakouts</td><td>Pumpkin seeds, chickpeas, eggs, shellfish</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Omega-3s</em></td><td>Support barrier lipids and help calm inflammation</td><td>Salmon, sardines, walnuts, chia, flaxseed</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Vitamin A</em></td><td>Supports skin renewal</td><td>Eggs, sweet potato, carrots, leafy greens</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Vitamin C</em></td><td>Needed to make collagen</td><td>Citrus, kiwi, bell peppers, berries</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Amino acids</em></td><td>Building blocks of skin structure</td><td>Eggs, fish, yogurt, lentils, tofu</td></tr></table>
<p>If absorption is impaired, skincare on top cannot fully make up for it. That is why I think of nutrition as the foundation and serums and creams as the support.</p>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">Food first, and be careful with doses</div><p>The US National Institutes of Health sets the daily upper limit for zinc at 40 mg for adults, from all sources, and for preformed vitamin A at 3,000 mcg. Too much zinc over time can lower copper levels, and too much preformed vitamin A during pregnancy can cause birth defects. I would speak to a doctor before taking either as a supplement.</p></div>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 5<br><em>Flat lay of nutrient-rich foods: salmon, avocado, eggs, berries</em></span></div>

<h2>Skin barrier healing is inside plus outside</h2>
<p>True repair takes both sides. For me that means:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>calming gut inflammation with regular meals, fibre and variety</li>
<li>supporting digestion and absorption</li>
<li>reducing stress load and protecting sleep</li>
<li>gentle, barrier-safe skincare</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">Healthy skin isn’t about doing more. It’s about supporting your body properly.</blockquote>

<h2>What I would do first</h2>
<p>I would not start with a strict protocol. These are the steps I would try first, one at a time, and give each a few weeks:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Eat more variety.</strong> Many different plants through the week, with plenty of fibre.</li>
<li><strong>Build a plate for the barrier.</strong> A protein, an omega-3 source and colourful fruit or vegetables.</li>
<li><strong>Keep highly processed foods occasional.</strong> They do not need to disappear, they just should not be the base.</li>
<li><strong>Protect sleep and stress.</strong> Both affect the gut and the skin.</li>
<li><strong>Keep skincare gentle.</strong> A mild cleanser, a moisturiser with <a href="/skin-guide/actives/ceramides/">ceramides</a>, SPF, and a pause on strong actives while your skin is reactive. <a href="/skin-guide/actives/centella-asiatica/">Centella</a> is a calming option.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">When I would see a doctor</div><p>If you have persistent eczema, severe or painful acne, or digestive symptoms such as ongoing pain, bloating, blood in the stool or unexplained weight loss, please see a doctor or dermatologist. I am not a medical professional, and this note is not a substitute for one.</p></div>

